<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Adrianople (1829)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1829">1829</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Treaty of Edirne (1829) · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1828-1829">Russo-Turkish War (1828–1829)</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Adrianople, signed on 14 September 1829 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a>, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1828-1829">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1828–29. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and much of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhaltsikhe">Akhaltsikhe</a>, and accepted Russian protection over Moldavia and Wallachia and autonomy for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. It also opened the Straits to merchant ships and led to the recognition of Greek independence in 1830.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-adrianople-1829.jpg" alt="Treaty of Adrianople (1829)" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moscow_Triumphal_Gates.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
